A||N||5||Anders Lundin||Which sensors control idle?||||5||
Z||000000||Anders Lundin||10-19-2001||05:46 AM||||I have an 87 GT, that is idling on 1500 I have checked the engine temperature sensor and the resistance changes from 1900 ohm to 600 ohm when temperature change from room temperature to working temp is this correct?<BR>Are there more sensors influencing the idle?<P>------------------<BR>||||reg||5
Z||000001||tgowens||10-19-2001||09:57 AM||||The Throttle Position Sensor (TPS) and the Idle Air Control (IAC) both can affect the idle speed. Both are located on the throttle body. There is a small hole on the top of the throttle body (usually plugged with a small metal plug about the size of a pencil eraser) with a screw which can be adjusted to adjust idle speed up or down. - it should idle around 1000.........<P>If you have an abnormally high idle it could be caused by a vacuum leak and I would suspect a faulty EGT tube first or check the small plastic vacuum lines that connect everything on the top (MAP sensor, EGR solenoid, etc...) to see if any are cracked or have come loose....<P>Hope this helps...<P>------------------<BR><A HREF="http://www.tgowens.com/fiero/index.html" TARGET=_blank><BR>    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/pennocks_sig.jpg">    </A><BR><A HREF="http://www.cfog-i.org" TARGET=_blank>Classic Fiero Owners Group International</A>||||reg||1
Z||000002||Indiana_resto_guy||10-19-2001||10:00 AM||||It may be several things! IAC valve, TPS sensor, MAP sensor,  the temp sensor you're checking which sounds good by the way, or a vacuum leak (EGR system been checked?).<BR>And a few others, I would check these first.<P>Good Luck!<BR>IRG||||reg||1
Z||000003||Joe Torma||10-19-2001||11:04 AM||||Yeah those are the sensors...sounds like you have a leak.<P>To set idle...<BR>-Ground out terminals in ADL connector<BR>-Put key in RUN<BR>-Let sit for a minute to allow the IAC to screw in.<BR>-Unplug IAC, turn off key<BR>-Unground ADL(???I forget, but pretty sure)<BR>-Start car and adjust the Idle screw to get it to run at like 500-600rpm.<BR>-Turn off car and plug IAC back in and you should be good to go.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Anders Lundin||10-20-2001||04:04 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Joe Torma:<BR><B>Yeah those are the sensors...sounds like you have a leak.<P>To set idle...<BR>-Ground out terminals in ADL connector<BR>-Put key in RUN<BR>-Let sit for a minute to allow the IAC to screw in.<BR>-Unplug IAC, turn off key<BR>-Unground ADL(???I forget, but pretty sure)<BR>-Start car and adjust the Idle screw to get it to run at like 500-600rpm.<BR>-Turn off car and plug IAC back in and you should be good to go.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>A few questions to this.<BR>ADL is that the sam as ALCL (assembly line communication link located close to the cigaret lighter?<BR>Should I ground all terminals?<BR>Is there an idle screw on the V6 engine?<BR>Do you mean start engine when you say -Put key in RUN?<BR>thanks||||reg||1
Z||000005||Anders Lundin||10-20-2001||04:21 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Indiana_resto_guy:<BR><B>It may be several things! IAC valve, TPS sensor, MAP sensor,  the temp sensor you're checking which sounds good by the way, or a vacuum leak (EGR system been checked?).<BR>And a few others, I would check these first.<P>Good Luck!<BR>IRG</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Thanks!<BR>Some additional info. I know that IAC is working(?)because if I drive on 5 gear without touching the accelerator pedal and let the rpm drop to 1000 and unclutch the idle shortly reaches 2000 rpm this shows open IAC to compensate, and the reverse when I use the engine to reduce speed an get 2000 rpm without touching the accelerator pedal and unclutch the idle drops shortly to 1000 rpm. It seems like the ECM wants a raised idle.<BR>||||reg||1
